    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i'm working on a low-fat mayo formulation and having issues with phase separation in the high-acid stages. can you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in stabilizing the oil-water interface when the pH is low?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin acts as a powerful emulsifier by possessing both hydrophilic (water-loving) and lipophilic (oil-loving) parts. At low pH, the hydration shell around the lecithin molecules remains effective, allowing the polar head groups to strongly interact with the aqueous phase while the non-polar tails embed themselves within the oil droplets. This dual nature creates a robust steric and electrostatic barrier at the oil-water interface, effectively preventing droplet coalescence and phase separation.",
      "source": "target",
