    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. but i'm seeing some micro-coalescence happening even with the lecithin. how does the acidity specifically affect the zeta potential of the droplets, and could that be causing the breakdown in the emulsion?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lowering the pH increases the protonation of the negatively charged head groups on the lecithin, which can reduce the magnitude of the negative surface charge. This reduction in negative charge diminishes the electrostatic repulsive forces between adjacent oil droplets. Consequently, the zeta potential becomes less negative, decreasing the electrical barrier stabilizing the droplets and making them more susceptible to attractive forces and micro-coalescence.",
      "source": "target",
